# novadeck kernel source pin — Phase 1
# The TARBALL + its sha256 is the canonical pin. Validated 2026-06-17; re-pinned 2026-07-25
# (7.1.5, HW-validated on Pocket S2 + Pocket ACE); re-pinned 2026-08-03 (7.1.6); re-pinned
# 2026-08-18 (7.2 — mainline, released 2026-08-16; NOT HW-validated yet, see the gate below);
# re-pinned 2026-08-27 (7.2.1); re-pinned 2026-09-03 (7.2.3, issue #86); re-pinned 2026-09-08 (7.2.4);
# re-pinned 2026-09-11 (7.2.5, issue #91 — NOT HW-validated yet, and it needs patch 0525, see below).
version:    7.2.5
url:        https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v7.x/linux-7.2.5.tar.xz
sha256:     55ddf0df8325d9dad96fcff7bd93977d22e3f50af06527572af59b77c7632b78
git_tag:    v7.2.5
git_commit: a300e35c0a4b4a38fb53742ea6e2a203c98ee523

# The sha256 above is the one kernel.org publishes in the SIGNED sha256sums.asc for v7.x, not a
# hash of whatever the CDN happened to serve — re-checked 2026-09-11. The .asc itself verifies as
# a GOOD signature from "Kernel.org checksum autosigner <autosigner@kernel.org>", fingerprint
# B886 8C80 BA62 A1FF FAF5 FDA9 632D 3A06 589D A6B1. That key carries no trust path here, so the
# load-bearing check is CONTINUITY: the SAME signed file still lists 7.2.4 as
# 01710ee01737dac492f1bae52becd057e08d20d11589089aa06accff415c28dd and 7.2.3 as
# 8ba259e8e7b13ec6ef0941c8a39ad90b24bd4a4d6c0010ba6bafb794550ecd03, byte-for-byte the hashes this
# pin carried and shipped before — so the file describing 7.2.5 is the same file that described
# the releases we already trusted. (Note: keys.openpgp.org serves this key STRIPPED of its user
# IDs and gpg then refuses it as "no public key"; keyserver.ubuntu.com serves it whole.)
# git_commit is the DEREFERENCED tag (`git ls-remote 'v7.2.5^{}'`), matching how the
# 7.1.5/7.1.6/7.2/7.2.1/7.2.3/7.2.4 lines were recorded; the annotated tag object is
# 4c440ba64342e015be9eb06f1ea824eb6e81e562 and is NOT what belongs here.
#
# 7.2 was MAINLINE with no point release; 7.2.1 was the first stable one, and 7.2.5 (2026-09-11)
# is this pin's fourth in-series step. Precedent from 7.1.3 -> 7.1.5 says a point release INSIDE a
# series is a pure pin edit — and 7.2.5 is the bump that proves why that is a prediction and not a
# licence to skip the gates. It is NOT a pure pin edit: it backports a drm/msm/dsi commit that
# blacks both SM8650 boards, and carrying patch 0525 is what makes it safe. See the DSI paragraph
# under the patch bullet below. Re-run every gate, every time.
#
# 7.2.5 HW gate: PARTLY RUN (2 of 4) as of 2026-09-11, off a dev card built from this pin at
# 513cb8f. The gate is the SAME four boards 7.2.4 was validated on, but they are no longer
# interchangeable — only the two SM8650 ones can exercise 0525 at all:
#   Pocket ACE  qcs8550-ayaneo-pocketace  panel 0051 ayaneo,pocket-ace-panel   VIDEO mode    PASS
#   Pocket S2   sm8650-ayaneo-ps2         panel 0066 ayaneo,wt0630-2k          VIDEO mode  <-0525 TODO
#   Pocket FIT  sm8650-konkr-pf           panel 0067 ayaneo,ar14-144hz         VIDEO mode  <-0525 PASS
#   Pocket Max  sm8250-mangmi-pocket-max  panel 0058 mangmi,pocket-max-panel   COMMAND mode        TODO
# WHICH BOARD PROVES WHAT, read off the DSI host's compatible on the device and not from the board
# name: `qcom,sm8650-dsi-ctrl` binds msm_dsi_6g_v2_9_host_ops (the runtime-reparenting ops, so 0525
# is live), `qcom,sm8550-dsi-ctrl` and the sm8250 one bind the plain 6g ops (0525 cannot fire in
# either direction). So ACE and Max are NEGATIVE CONTROLS here: they prove nothing else in 7.2.5
# broke display bring-up, and nothing at all about the fix.
#   Pocket FIT, 2026-09-11: panel up 1080x1920, backlight on, gamescope on DSI-1, Steam up, no
#     failed units, and ZERO hits for the regression's signature ("Failed to set rate pixel clk",
#     "rcg didn't update", "Turning OFF PHY while PLL is on"). chipone-tddi touch bound, no
#     i2c/geni errors, no SMMU faults.
#   Pocket ACE, 2026-09-11: panel up 1080x1620, backlight on, session up, no failed units, no
#     i2c/geni errors. Three backlight round-trips through the sy7758 (an I2C device behind geni,
#     /soc@0/geniqup@ac0000/i2c@a80000/sy7758@2e) produced zero kernel output — which tests the
#     7.2.5 `i2c: qcom-geni` frequency-table change, NOT the DSI DCS path, since that backlight
#     does not go through the DSI host. A DSI-backlight panel is still needed to exercise that.
#   The NEGATIVE CONTROL WAS NOT RUN: no one booted 7.2.5 WITHOUT 0525 to watch Pocket FIT go
#   black. The reasoning that it would (rounding against XO can only return ~19.2 MHz, which is
#   then multiplied into the PHY bit-clock request) is sound but it is reasoning, not a measurement.
# Both DSI modes on purpose: 0517's premise is video-mode-specific, so the command-mode panel is
# there to prove it did not regress the other path, not to exercise it. Four of 21 boards, and
# three SoCs (qcs8550 / sm8650 / sm8250) — the untested 17 are still untested. Note also that a
# new Image invalidates the whole `/lib/modules/<ver>` tree (module BTF), so testing it means
# flashing a card built from this pin, not swapping /boot/Image on an existing one.
#
# One observation from the ACE boot, NOT a 7.2.5 defect: a bounded burst of 10 arm-smmu context
# faults at ~0.77s, all cbfrsynra=0x1c00 (the MDSS node — `iommus = <&apps_smmu 0x1c00 0x2>`),
# inside a 0.4 ms window, none after, and all of them BEFORE the panel rails come up at 0.854s.
# The bootloader continuous-splash handoff, i.e. what dpu-quiesce-boot-scanout.patch is for.
# CONFIRMED not bump fallout: 7.2.5 changes zero iommu/smmu files and exactly one drm/msm file.
# Pocket FIT shows ZERO such faults on the same kernel, so it is board-specific, not general.
# STILL OPEN: whether it predates our own 0514/0518-0524 additions. Nobody has checked.
#
# 7.2.4 WAS HW-validated, on 2026-09-09, on exactly those four boards (with 0515-0517), display up
# and no issues reported. That is the fallback if 7.2.5 fails its gate: revert this pin, not the
# patch set — 0525 is inert on 7.2.4, whose dsi_calc_clk_rate_6g() does no rounding at all, so it
# would fail to apply and must come out with the pin.

# Config validation (kernel/*.config) against THIS tree, RE-RUN 2026-09-11 for the 7.2.5 re-pin
# (numbers below are 7.2.5's; the 7.2.4 run they replace found 426/0-missing and 80 patches/26 fuzz,
# the 7.2.3 one 429/0-missing and 71 patches/26 fuzz, the 7.2.1 one 428/0-missing and 71 patches/26
# fuzz, and the 7.2 one 427/1-missing and 69 patches/21 fuzz):
#   - all 426 referenced CONFIG_* symbols (kernel.config + trim-platforms.config) exist in the
#     PATCHED 7.2.5 tree, with NONE missing. The count did not move at this bump: 426 at 7.2.4, 426
#     here, and neither fragment was edited. Historically it moves for OUR reasons, not upstream's —
#     429 -> 426 at 7.2.4 when three dead trim-platforms directives were deleted, and 428 -> 429 at
#     7.2.3 when b4ddf2d added AYANEO_SERIAL_MCU, a symbol our OWN
#     1005-misc-add-ayaneo-serial-mcu.patch defines, which is exactly why the sweep must run
#     against the patched tree and not a pristine one. Sweep: strip `CONFIG_x=` and
#     `# CONFIG_x is not set` to bare names, diff against every `config`/`menuconfig` name in the
#     tree's Kconfig files.
#     The one 7.2 casualty stays deleted and its note is still why — see kernel.config's header:
#       READ_ONLY_THP_FOR_FS -> removed upstream in 7.2; file THP is unconditional now.
#     SCHED_CACHE (added 2026-08-22) still resolves the same way (init/Kconfig, `default y`,
#     `depends on SMP`) — the pin records an inherited default rather than changing one.
#   - all 88 out-of-tree patches in kernel/patches/ apply to 7.2.5 with ZERO rejects, applied
#     CUMULATIVELY in lexical order, which is the only order that means anything (see below).
#     The patch count moved 80 -> 87 since the 7.2.4 re-pin and NOT because of this bump: 0514 and
#     0518-0524 (the drm/msm VM_BIND shared-VM fallback backport) were added on main in the meantime.
#     87 -> 88 IS this bump: 0525, and it is the whole story of this re-pin — see the DSI paragraph
#     below. 26 hunks need fuzz; kernel/build.sh passes no -F0, so patch's default fuzz=2 accepts
#     them. The fuzz count CLIMBS with every point release — when it reaches 2 on a hunk that
#     matters, patch will start applying it in the wrong place silently. So the raw total is the
#     WRONG instrument: 26 was also 7.2.1's, 7.2.3's and 7.2.4's total, and a hunk sliding 1 -> 2
#     while another slid 2 -> 1 would leave it unmoved. The 7.2.5 run therefore replayed the
#     identical drill against a pristine 7.2.4 tree and diffed the PER-HUNK profile (patch name +
#     hunk + fuzz level): the two are byte-identical, 16 hunks at fuzz 1 and 10 at fuzz 2, same
#     patches on both sides. Nothing climbed. 0525 was then added and the drill replayed in the
#     other axis — two pristine 7.2.5 trees, 87 patches on one and 88 on the other — and the
#     profiles are again byte-identical at 16/10, so 0525 applied at fuzz 0 AND moved no existing
#     hunk. "0 rejects" alone does not prove a patch landed, so that run also asserted 0525's code
#     is present in the 88-patch tree (`byte_intf_clk_div_2` in the struct, in the byte-intf branch
#     and in host_power_on; no clk_round_rate left in dsi_calc_clk_rate_6g) and absent from the
#     87-patch one.
#   - independent corroboration, and the cheapest signal in this whole drill: the pure upstream delta
#     read straight off the INCREMENTAL patch, which is far cheaper than diffing two patched trees
#     and says the same thing (https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v7.x/incr/patch-7.2.4-5.xz —
#     take the `+++ b/` paths). 7.2.4 -> 7.2.5 changes 462 files; our patches touch 153; the
#     INTERSECTION IS TWO FILES, and reading them is what turned this bump from routine into the
#     one that needed a new patch. THIS IS THE BULLET THAT EARNED ITS KEEP — it named the problem
#     before a single hunk was read, and it is the ONLY gate that found it: the fuzz profile could
#     not, because upstream changed a function none of our patches contexts on, and the config
#     sweep could not, because no symbol moved. Run it FIRST on every bump.
#       drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c  — ours: 0003, 0005, 0512.   THE BLOCKER. 7.2.5 backports
#         d152b582d852 ("drm/msm/dsi: round 6G byte clock rate to the PLL-achievable value",
#         6cd33b6f4155 upstream), which rounds the byte clock in dsi_calc_clk_rate_6g() — i.e.
#         before the DSI 6G v2.9 hosts reparent the byte RCG from XO to the PHY PLL. The rounded-
#         against-XO rate lands in the PHY bit clock request and the PLL is programmed wrong:
#         "Failed to set rate pixel clk, -22" and a dead panel. v2.9 is exactly sm8650 + kaanapali
#         in dsi_cfg.c, so for us it is Pocket S2 and Pocket FIT — two of the four boards 7.2.4
#         was validated on. Patch 0525 carries Dmitry Baryshkov's fix (move the rounding into
#         dsi_link_clk_set_rate_6g(), after the reparent). NOT the competing straight revert, which
#         loses the rounding entirely. None of 0003/0005/0512 owns any line the upstream commit or
#         the fix touches, verified by diffing the patched tree against the pristine one.
#       drivers/pmdomain/qcom/rpmhpd.c      — ours: 0121.   BENIGN, but it had to be read to know
#         that. Upstream reverts the MXC/MMCX additions to eliza_rpmhpds[]; our 0121 adds
#         presync_floor/skip_retention_level to the gmxc struct ABOVE that table and only uses the
#         "/* Eliza RPMH powerdomains */" comment as trailing context, which the revert leaves
#         alone. Eliza is not one of our three SoCs either way.
#     The other question a bump raises: nothing of OURS landed upstream this release. Both
#     intersecting changes are upstream's own, and all 88 patches applied forward, so no patch
#     becomes a deletion candidate at 7.2.5.
#   - what the intersection CANNOT see, and therefore what the HW gate is actually for: 7.2.5 also
#     changes drivers we depend on but carry no patch against, so nothing offline will flag them.
#     Worth a look if the gate turns up something odd:
#       i2c: qcom-geni: update frequency table to fix timing parameters   — every I2C touchscreen
#         and DDIC on these boards sits behind geni. A timing change here is the most plausible
#         source of a "touch works but flakes" or backlight-write result on a board that was fine.
#       soc: qcom: geni-se: use HW PROG_RAM_DEPTH to validate firmware size
#       media: qcom: iris: four fixes (gen1 GOP_SIZE cap, disable_irq on power-off, state-change
#         log, bus_info) — iris is our =m video decoder on all three SoCs, so exercise it with
#         ffmpeg v4l2m2m and NOT Steam (see the iris/venus node interlock in build.sh's comments).
#       remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_pas, usb: typec/tcpm qcom-pmic — routine stable fixes, listed only so
#         the next reader knows they were looked at and not missed.
#   - `patch unexpectedly ends in middle of line` x4 is NOT a reject: patches 0004 (IFPC) and 0203
#     (S16_LE) have no trailing newline. Both hunks succeed. Pre-existing, unrelated to any bump.
#   - the fragments were then MERGED for real inside build/Dockerfile (`merge_config.sh -m
#     defconfig kernel/*.config` + `make olddefconfig`, trim-platforms.config included) and every
#     requested symbol asserted to have kept its requested value. All 426 came out right and zero
#     symbols came out wrong — including DEBUG_INFO_BTF/SCHED_CLASS_EXT, which resolve correctly
#     here because the container ships `dwarves` (pahole v1.31).
#   - that assertion is also what caught the one real defect the 7.2.4 re-pin surfaced, in OUR tree
#     rather than upstream's. It is kept here because the mechanism is permanent, not because it
#     recurred: at 7.2.5 the same run reports 46 cut and nothing redundant, unchanged.
#     Three symbols trim-platforms.config asked to turn off came back
#     `undef` (absent from .config) instead of `n`: ARCH_TESLA_FSD and ARCH_ARTPEC both
#     `depends on ARCH_EXYNOS`, and DRM_NOUVEAU_BACKLIGHT `depends on DRM_NOUVEAU` — all three
#     gates that the same file already cuts, so all three lines cut nothing. They were removed
#     (46 directives now, floor is 40) and build.sh now counts `n` and `undef` apart so the next
#     one is visible in the build log instead of silent. `undef` still PASSES — it is off either
#     way — so an assertion that demands the literal `# ... is not set` line reports false
#     failures. Read the two states as different facts, not as pass/fail.
#     Re-verified after the deletion: `make kernel` reports `trim-platforms: 46 symbols confirmed
#     disabled` with no redundant list, and all 426 requested symbols still land on their
#     requested values. So every directive left in that file is load-bearing.
#   - and the whole thing was then BUILT: `make kernel` against 7.2.5 on 2026-09-11, clean, all 88
#     patches applied including 0525, `trim-platforms: 46 symbols confirmed disabled` with no
#     redundant list, Image + all 21 board DTBs + 946 modules under /lib/modules/7.2.5. That is the
#     gate the offline drill cannot give you. 47 compiler warnings; the ones that were read are in
#     OUR out-of-tree drivers (qcom-hv-haptics.c's bare `rc=%d` in sweep_run_store, and its
#     missing-prototype set), i.e. in files 7.2.5 does not touch, so they cannot be bump fallout.
#     They were not diffed against a 7.2.4 build log — no such log was kept — so treat "47" as a
#     number to compare against next time, not as a verified-unchanged one.
#     7.2.4 built the same way on 2026-09-08, and a dev card off that build booted four boards
#     clean on 2026-09-09. 7.2.5 has NOT been on hardware.
#
# HOW TO RE-RUN THIS VALIDATION, and the mistake that makes it lie: patches must be applied
# cumulatively to ONE tree, exactly as kernel/build.sh does. Dry-running each patch independently
# against a pristine tree reports ~8 false failures, because several patches depend on files an
# earlier patch CREATES (1002 patches the qcom-hv-haptics.c that 1000 adds; 1003 patches the
# rsinput.c that 0031 adds) and the drm/panel patches all extend one Makefile, each one's
# context containing the line its predecessor added. Zero of those are real.
#
# Corrections applied during the 7.1.5 validation, still current and still load-bearing in
# kernel.config — do not "restore" the left-hand names:
#   CRYPTO_MICHAEL_MIC  -> DROPPED (upstream removed it from crypto/Kconfig; mac80211 carries its
#                          own michael_mic, so TKIP is unaffected). Was already a dead symbol in
#                          7.1.3 and silently ignored by kconfig.
#   MFD_QCOM_SPMI_PMIC  -> MFD_SPMI_PMIC
#   PHY_QCOM_SNPS_EUSB2 -> PHY_SNPS_EUSB2 (+ PHY_QCOM_EUSB2_REPEATER)
#   SM_DISPCC_8650      -> SM_DISPCC_8550 (SM8650 reuses the 8550 display clock controller;
#                          no separate _8650 dispcc upstream — symbols jump 8550 -> 8750)
#
# --- What 7.2 took over from us, and what that cost ---
# Two of our patches went upstream in 7.2 and were DELETED here rather than carried:
#   0101 (sm8550 DDR/LLCC/L3 CPU bandwidth scaling) — landed verbatim; all 11 hunks read as
#         already-applied against 7.2's sm8550.dtsi. Pure deletion, no other change.
#   0065 (sgm3804 panel-power regulator) — landed as a REWRITE, not our version, and with an
#         INCOMPATIBLE DT binding. Upstream's driver registers TWO rails from `pos`/`neg` child
#         nodes each carrying `enable-gpios`; ours was one rail with both GPIOs in `reset-gpios`
#         on the parent, hardcoding 5.2V (reg 0x0c) over I2C while telling the framework 5.0V.
#         Keeping both was not an option: same compatible, same file path, duplicate Kconfig
#         symbol. So the binding was migrated across all six board nodes and the panels that
#         consume it — see the HW gate below, this is the risky half of the bump.
#
# HW GATE, PARTLY RUN (3 of 7) as of 2026-09-09. The sgm3804 migration changes how panel power is
# brought up on Pocket ACE / DMG / DS / S1K / S2K, Pocket S2 and konkr-pf. Three of those seven —
# Pocket ACE, Pocket S2 and konkr-pf (Pocket FIT) — came up clean on the 2026-09-09 card, which is
# the failure this gate was written to catch (a lit backlight over a black panel, AVEE dead). DMG,
# DS, S1K and S2K are STILL UNTESTED, and they are the ones to try next:
#   - `avdd-supply` now points at the positive rail only; the negative rail is a SECOND regulator
#     that nothing enabled before, so every panel driver that used to get both rails from one
#     `avdd` now takes an `avee` supply too (patches 0051/0052/0054/0066/0067/0068). A board left
#     un-migrated would come up with AVEE dead — a lit backlight over a black panel.
#   - the rails are pinned to 5200000 uV, NOT the 5000000 the old DTS asked for. 5.2V is what the
#     hardware was actually running: our driver ignored the DT constraint and wrote selector 0x0c,
#     which is 4.0V + 12*100mV on upstream's linear range. Asking for 5.0V here would be a real,
#     silent voltage change dressed up as a no-op.
#   - upstream's driver declares enable_time = 40ms per rail; ours had none. regulator_bulk_enable
#     is async so both rails come up in parallel, but panel prepare is now ~40ms slower.
#
# The migration above was CROSS-CHECKED against the not-yet-merged upstream Pocket S2 display
# series (Neil Armstrong, Linaro), which is the reference for how these nodes are meant to look:
#   [v5] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8650-ayaneo-pocket-s2: add display nodes -- 2026-07-28, state `new`
#   https://patchwork.kernel.org/project/linux-arm-msm/patch/20260728-topic-sm8650-ayaneo-pocket-s2-display-dt-v5-1-892ab9bcecfe@linaro.org/
# (lore.kernel.org is behind an Anubis proof-of-work wall and cannot be fetched; patchwork's
# /api/1.2/ and /raw/ endpoints serve the same mail and are not gated. Use those.)
# It independently confirms 5200000 uV on both rails and the same tlmm 59=pos / 58=neg order we
# already had, and it carries three properties this tree was missing, now added to all six nodes:
#   vin-supply = <&vph_pwr>       the chip's input rail (label reachable on every board with an
#                                 sgm3804: qcs8550-ayaneo-pocket-common.dtsi + sm8650-ayaneo-common.dtsi)
#   regulator-active-discharge = <1>   restores the discharge bits our old driver wrote to reg 0x03
#                                 unconditionally; upstream only writes them when DT asks
#   regulator-boot-on             the rail can already be up at handoff (bootloader splash)
#
# WHERE WE STILL DIVERGE, deliberately: upstream names the panel's rails `vsp-supply`/`vsn-supply`
# and drives the S2 panel with a generic `renesas,r63419` driver (compatible "ayaneo,wt0630-2k",
# "renesas,r63419"). Neither that driver nor that series is in 7.2, so our six panel drivers stay
# and keep their own `avdd`/`avee` names. If we ever drop them for the upstream r63419 driver, the
# DTS supply names have to become vsp/vsn at the same time — they are a driver-side contract.
